1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,646
Logs explain discrete events, metrics summarize behavior over time, and traces connect

2
00:00:05,646 --> 00:00:07,528
work across service boundaries.

3
00:00:07,678 --> 00:00:13,394
Use correlation identifiers and structured fields while avoiding credentials and unnecessary customer

4
00:00:13,394 --> 00:00:13,870
data.

5
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:21,151
Measure request rates, errors, latency distributions, pool waits, database contention, and business

6
00:00:21,151 --> 00:00:21,746
outcomes.

7
00:00:21,896 --> 00:00:24,505
Averages alone can hide slow-tail behavior.

8
00:00:24,655 --> 00:00:30,715
Containerization packages runtime dependencies but does not make an application operationally complete.

9
00:00:30,865 --> 00:00:38,040
Configure resource limits, health checks, graceful shutdown, secret injection, database backups, and

10
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:39,236
deployment rollback.

11
00:00:39,386 --> 00:00:42,425
A successful build is different from a successful rollout.

12
00:00:42,575 --> 00:00:46,425
Readiness should reflect whether the instance can serve its intended traffic.

13
00:00:46,575 --> 00:00:50,558
The demo exposes health and info through Actuator and binds to loopback.

14
00:00:50,708 --> 00:00:57,718
PostgreSQL configuration and migrations prepare the next deployment step, but production authentication,

15
00:00:57,718 --> 00:01:03,559
broker delivery, telemetry export, and load-tested capacity remain explicit extensions.

16
00:01:03,709 --> 00:01:08,505
A pending-order count and age metric would reveal stuck reconciliation better than

17
00:01:08,505 --> 00:01:10,103
HTTP status counts alone.

18
00:01:10,253 --> 00:01:15,161
Create a container image, run each service with a separate PostgreSQL database,

19
00:01:15,161 --> 00:01:17,206
and load-test a fixed workload.

20
00:01:17,356 --> 00:01:25,073
Record hardware, dataset size, concurrency, duration, throughput, error rate, and latency percentiles.

21
00:01:25,223 --> 00:01:29,755
Compare measurements under the same conditions and avoid claiming production capacity from

22
00:01:29,755 --> 00:01:30,888
a laptop test.

23
00:01:31,038 --> 00:01:34,402
Include dependency and database limits in the explanation.

24
00:01:34,552 --> 00:01:39,243
Verify restore procedures and schema compatibility as part of recovery, not only

25
00:01:39,243 --> 00:01:41,198
that a backup file exists.

26
00:01:41,348 --> 00:01:42,019
Question.

27
00:01:42,169 --> 00:01:44,621
Why is p95 useful?

28
00:01:44,771 --> 00:01:45,348
Answer.

29
00:01:45,498 --> 00:01:49,903
It describes the latency below which 95 percent of measured requests fall,

30
00:01:49,903 --> 00:01:52,473
revealing tail behavior hidden by the mean.

31
00:01:52,623 --> 00:01:53,294
Question.

32
00:01:53,444 --> 00:01:56,442
What should a deployment rollback consider besides code?

33
00:01:56,592 --> 00:01:57,168
Answer.

34
00:01:57,318 --> 00:02:03,321
Schema compatibility, configuration, external effects, queued events, and data written by the

35
00:02:03,321 --> 00:02:04,322
newer version.
